<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Lagos Colony</h1><p class="st-meta">1862 CE – 1906 CE · capital Lagos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British colony formed when the island town of Lagos was annexed in 1861, which grew into the commercial heart of what became Nigeria.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1851 British warships bombarded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lagos">Lagos</a>, deposed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosoko">Kosoko</a> of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> and restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akitoye">Akitoye</a>, ostensibly to end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. A British consul then dominated affairs, and in August 1861 Akitoye's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dosunmu">Dosunmu</a> signed a treaty ceding Lagos to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> under pressure. It was organised as a crown colony in 1862.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lagos">Lagos</a> became the chief port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bight-of-benin">Bight of Benin</a>, exporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> and later cocoa. Its society included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> townspeople, Saro from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agudas">Afro-Brazilian returnees</a>, who built churches, schools and newspapers. Administrator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hawley-glover">John Hawley Glover</a> extended influence inland in the 1860s. From 1874 to 1886 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> was governed as part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony</a>, then became separate, and from 1895 a railway was built towards <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ibadan">Ibadan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1906 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lagos">Lagos</a> was merged with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-southern-nigeria">Protectorate of Southern Nigeria</a>, and it became the capital of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colonial-nigeria">Colonial Nigeria</a> in 1914. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> remained the federal capital until 1991 and is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria's</a> largest city.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lagos-colony.jpg" alt="Lagos Colony" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lagos_Colony_(1888%E2%80%931906).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1862 CE – 1906 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lagos</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John Hawley Glover, Gilbert Carter</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Islam, Yoruba religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Yoruba</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony of Lagos, Lagos and Protectorate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1884&amp;focus=lagos-colony" aria-label="Lagos Colony on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4774 1936 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lagos Colony lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5094 2192L5126 2155M5094 2192L5108 2158M5094 2192L5094 2192M5094 2192L4997 651M5094 2192L4634 2117M5094 2192L4995 2226M5094 2192L5094 2192M5094 2192L5144 2163"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5126 2155h0M5108 2158h0M5094 2192h0M4997 651h0M4634 2117h0M4995 2226h0M5094 2192h0M5144 2163h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5094 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5094 2192h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lagos. <a href="/atlas?year=1884&amp;focus=lagos-colony">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
